.Dd January 27, 2026
.Dt KMEM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kmem
.Nd kernel virtual memory access device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn open "/dev/kmem" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device provides access to the kernel virtual address space.
The file offset corresponds to the kernel virtual address.
.Pp
Reading from
.Nm
returns the contents of the kernel memory at the specified address.
Writing to
.Nm
writes to the kernel memory at the specified address.
.Pp
Access to
.Nm
is restricted to privileged processes (effective user ID 0) and is further subject to
security policies configured via
.Xr sysctl 8 .
.Sh SECURITY
The
.Nm
device is a critical security interface.
Improper use can crash the system or compromise security.
.Pp
The following
.Xr sysctl 8
variables control access:
.Bl -tag -width ".Va kern.kmem_allow_write"
.It Va kern.kmem_allow_read
Allow reading from
.Nm .
Default is 1 (allowed).
.It Va kern.kmem_allow_write
Allow writing to
.Nm .
Default is 0 (denied).
.El
.Pp
If the system securelevel
.Pq Va kern.securelevel
is greater than 0, access to
.Nm
may be completely disabled or restricted to read-only regardless of the above settings.
.Pp
Memory mapping
.Pq Xr mmap 2
of
.Nm
is explicitly disallowed to prevent bypassing kernel security mechanisms.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/kmem" -compact
.It Pa /dev/kmem
Kernel virtual memory device.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
Permission denied.
The process does not have sufficient privileges, or the operation is disallowed by policy or securelevel.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
Bad address.
The specified address is not a valid kernel virtual address or caused a page fault.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Invalid argument.
Attempted to
.Xr mmap 2
the device.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Inappropriate I/O control operation.
.Xr ioctl 2
is not supported.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr mem 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
device appeared in the first version of UNIX.
This implementation is specific to Substrate OS.
